        "title": "Housing Markets and Policy",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Housing markets are economically unusual: inelastic supply (building takes years), inelastic demand (people need shelter), durable assets (buildings last 50-100+ years), and location-specific (you cannot move a unit to where demand is higher). These characteristics combine to make housing markets volatile and policy interventions frequently counterproductive."
